  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe main story for the long term period will be oppressive heat for \nparts of the area, primarily on Thursday and Friday. At this time, \nheat index values are generally expected to range from 100 to 107, \nso additional Heat Advisories may be needed for parts of the area \nboth days. Actual air temperatures will range from the mid-90s to \naround 100 degrees, so conditions will be downright oppressive and \neven hazardous for vulnerable groups (those without air \nconditioning, outdoor workers, babies, the elderly, and those with \ncertain medical conditions).\n\nArea-wide PoPs return on Friday as an amplifying ridge over the West \nforces expansive troughing over the East. Surface-based instability \nshould be bountiful (it is summer in the Southeast, after all) so \nbarring significant capping, at least isolated to scattered \nconvection should occur across the area. Strong mid-/upper-level \nflow associated with the trough looks to support deep-layer shear \nperhaps as far south as north Georgia. Some of the longer-range CAMs \n(available on the NSSL website) depict a few clusters of storms \ndeveloping across the Southeast on Thursday and Friday, although \ntheir placement and evolution vary widely as predictability is very \nlow. For what it's worth, NCAR medium-range AI NWP convective hazard \nforecasts and ML products have signals of severe potential. Thus, it \nwill be wise to monitor trends in severe weather parameters with \nsubsequent model runs, as upscale growth in convection could bring \nsevere weather potential to north and central Georgia.\n\nNBM guidance suggests a front (largely a moisture gradient) pushing \nthrough Saturday into Sunday. This feature is currently expected to \nlower PoPs markedly Sunday and Monday. While heat index values will \ndecrease both days, high temperatures are likely to remain in the \n90s for much of the area.",
